NAME
       basename, dirname - split a path into its last part and the rest

SYNOPSIS
       #include <libgen.h>

       char *basename(char *path);
       char *dirname(char *path);

DESCRIPTION
       basename returns the last component of the path; dirname
       returns everything before it. Trailing slashes are ignored.

         path            dirname    basename
         /usr/lib/x      /usr/lib   x
         /usr/lib/       /usr       lib
         file            .          file
         /               /          /
         ""              .          .

       Both may write to the string they are given and may return a
       pointer into it, so pass a copy if the original still matters.
       The result may also point at static storage that the next call
       overwrites.

RETURN VALUE
       A pointer to the component. Never NULL.

SEE ALSO
       basename(1), dirname(1), string(3)
